        <div class="ts-hdive__textcol">
          <div class="ts-hdive__panel">
            <h2 class="ts-hdive__title ts-hdive__title--aside">RGB scanning made simple</h2>
            <p class="ts-hdive__sub ts-hdive__sub--aside">The way high-end lab scanners have always got their colour, now automatic on your own camera.</p>

            <h3 class="ts-hdive__ptitle ts-hdive__ptitle--sub">The best colour a camera scan can give you</h3>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">What you just watched is three separate exposures of one frame coming together. The negative was photographed once under red light, once under green and once under blue, and each of those exposures read a single dye layer of the film on its own.</p>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">You did not line any of it up. toneScan Studio found the three frames sitting in your capture folder, recognised them as one shot, matched their exposure channel by channel and combined them into the finished picture.</p>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">It is the same principle the best lab scanners have used for years, and until now it meant lab hardware. All it takes here is a folder of RAW files and a light source that can change colour.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="ts-hdive__panel">
            <h3 class="ts-hdive__ptitle">Why it beats white light</h3>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">Under white light your sensor sees all three dye layers at once, through its own colour filters, and the channels end up bleeding into each other.</p>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">Separating the exposures keeps each layer measured largely on its own, so there is far less crosstalk to unpick afterwards and far more room in the shadows before colour starts to fall apart.</p>
          </div>

